          Digital Sets New Standard in System Support with
                        Priority Service Plan


      ...Innovative pricing and packaging approach dramatically
        simplifies the evaluation and purchase of services...


MAYNARD, Mass. -- Nov. 11, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced an innovative program that better enables customers 
to evaluate and purchase the right level of service for their 
computing environments.  Called the Digital Priority Service Plan, 
it brings consumer-level simplicity to the support of Digital's 
Alpha and Prioris systems and add-on products.

     The industry-leading plan offers innovative packaging and 
pricing that allow Digital and its resellers, at the point of 
product sale, to quickly determine the service price of an entire 
configuration -- regardless of the number of line items -- with a 
single table look-up.  The plan eliminates the complexity usually 
associated with the purchase and sale of system service.  It is 
designed to boost both customer and service reseller satisfaction, 
and to make it easier for them to do business with Digital.  

     In addition, the new service packages typically offer most 
customers a three-to-six percent savings over the traditional 
a-la-carte approach to buying services.  They also provide resellers 
with a [greater than] 95 percent reduction in the time spent 
computing and quoting service prices.  

     "The Priority Service Plan represents a major breakthrough in 
the way Digital sells services, offering many benefits to Digital 
end users and service resellers," said Tom Iannotti, sales and 
marketing vice president of Digital's Multivendor Customer Services 
Business Unit.  "The plan makes it dramatically easier and quicker 
for end[-]users to evaluate different service levels and balance 
their costs against the desired level of application availability.   

     "With its unique price-banding approach," added Iannotti, "the 
plan also makes Digital the easiest partner with whom to sell 
services and achieve customer satisfaction.  Its building-block 
design makes it easy to augment individual service plans to meet 
unique customer needs."

     Pioneer Standard Electronics is among the Digital service 
resellers that are already selling the new service packages.  "With 
the Priority Service Plan," said Wendy Stevens, Pioneer's corporate 
marketing manager, "our sales people can work with end users to 
easily determine the level of service that best meets both their 
application availability needs and budget constraints."

     Wyle Electronics and Avnet CMG have had a similarly positive 
experience with the new packages.  "We no longer need to educate 
customers about warranty variances across the different products in 
a configuration," said Bob Mitten, Wyle's services marketing 
manager. "The Priority Service Plan enables us to quickly price a 
consistent level of service for an entire configuration -- with only 
a single part number.  Our customers are going to love it." 

     Jami Laurence, Avnet CMG's business development manager, said, 
"The Priority Service Plan enables us to quote the right level of 
services quickly.  As a result, we can increase our total revenue 
both from a transaction and customer satisfaction standpoint, 
without increasing our cost of sales."

     The Digital Priority Service Plan offers three levels of 
combined  hardware and software service packages: Priority, 
Priority-Plus, and Priority-Premier.  Customers simply choose the 
package that best suits their environment and needs.  

     The plan is available now in the U.S. and the Asia Pacific 
region, and will be available worldwide within 60 to 90 days.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is a world leader in open client/
server solutions from personal computers to integrated worldwide 
information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha and Intel platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
