LAS VEGAS, Nev., Nov. 18, 1996 -- New desktop, compatibility, 
mobile, and network computing solutions, based on Digital 
Semiconductor's top-performing Alpha and StrongARM microprocessors 
and PCI multimedia chips, lead the Digital Semiconductor exhibit at 
Fall COMDEX '96, today through Friday at the Las Vegas Convention 
Center.

     More than 20 technology partners and OEM customers (See Booth 
Guide) are joining Digital Semiconductor to welcome visitors as they 
"Enter the Alpha Age" at Booth L4602, North Hall.  Visitors can 
sample price- competitive, performance-focused systems that they can 
buy today, as well as preview soon-to-be-available products for a 
broad range of computing environments.

     Alpha partners Mitsubishi Electric, Samsung Electronics, and 
VLSI Technology are present to discuss their roles and product plans 
that will drive the Alpha architecture into the mainstream corporate 
PC market.  Electronics giants Mitsubishi and Samsung are licensed 
by Digital Semiconductor to manufacture and market Alpha chips, 
while VLSI will produce system logic support chips for future Alpha 
microprocessors.

ALPHA PAVILION

     The Alpha Pavilion, Digital Semiconductor's booth headliner, 
demonstrates Alpha performance, Windows compatibility, and market 
momentum with the following major activities:

Alpha Performance Theater

An Alpha PC164-500MHz system and a 200MHz Pentium Pro system square 
off in a side-by-side performance comparison, running popular 
Windows NT visual computing applications for 3D animation, content 
creation, and video editing.

Compatibility Test Drive Theater

Show-goers are invited to sample the speed of Digital's breakthrough 
Digital FX!32 translation software on 14 Alpha PC164 systems running 
Microsoft Office 95, Adobe Photoshop, games, and other applications.  
Digital FX!32 software enables Alpha systems to run 32-bit Windows 
and Windows NT applications as fast or faster than on high-end x86 
systems.  

Alpha Technology
  
A demonstration of digital versatile disk (DVD) realtime video 
playback on an Alpha PC shows the power of Alpha for performing 
realtime video decompression without special accelerators.  Also, 
a new PC 164LX motherboard design for the Alpha 21164 microprocessor 
shows a new chipset capable of supporting Alpha processors at clock 
rates up to 700MHz.  

Alpha Customers
  
Alpha systems ranging from sub-$3,000 PCs to dual-processor, 500MHz 
workstations, built by a full complement of independent Alpha system 
manufacturers, demonstrate popular Windows NT image rendering, 3-D 
animation, CAD/CAM, and desktop publishing applications.


EMERGING TECHNOLOGIES

Under the Emerging Technologies banner, StrongARM microprocessor and 
video/multimedia demonstrations focus on low-cost, high-performance 
solutions in mobile, networked, and PC-based environments:

Handheld Computing

Digital Semiconductor's StrongARM microprocessors provide the 
computing power for the battery-operated Apple MessagePad 2000 
handheld computer.

Internet Appliances

High-performance StrongARM chips drive Wyse Technology's new Winterm 
2000 thin-client systems, plus newly announced Internet terminals 
from Lucky Goldstar Electronics Ltd. and Boundless Technology, Inc.

PC Videoconferencing

A live ISDN videoconference between Pentium PCs demonstrates a 
market-ready solution using Digital Semiconductor's low-cost 21230 
video codec chip for H.320 standard-compliant, PC-based 
videoconferencing.  

MPEG-1 Video Solutions

Digital Semiconductor and multimedia customers Futuretel, OmniMedia, 
Vitec, Visionetics International, and Ulead Systems demonstrate 
MPEG-1 compression-decompression cards, video authoring/editing 
systems, and software using the 21230 video codec chip.

     Digital Semiconductor will also display its latest 10-100 
megabit-per-second Ethernet networking products for PCI and Cardbus 
applications, as well as PCI-to-PCI bridge chips for expanding PC 
and server I/O capacity.

"Strongest Showing Ever"

     "Digital Semiconductor has brought to Fall COMDEX its strongest 
showing ever in terms of advanced technology, market-ready products 
and solutions, and third-party support," said William N. Johnson, 
Digital Semiconductor's vice president, Semiconductor Business 
Segments.  "Our PCI peripheral chips continue to dominate the 
networking and bridge markets and to push PC video performance, 
while our Alpha and StrongARM products are gathering momentum in 
volume markets.  In addition, our customers continue to produce 
award-winning system solutions that lead the industry in performance 
and innovation."

