            Digital on Solid Ground -- and in the Air --
                          at Fall Comdex'96


        Windows NT Enterprise Solutions, the Power of Alpha,
       AltaVista Airship Dominate DIGITAL's Presence at COMDEX

LAS VEGAS, Nev., November 18, 1996 --  Digital Equipment Corporation 
arrived at Fall COMDEX '96 with its broad range of industry-leading 
solutions for rapidly emerging Windows NT enterprise and Internet 
requirements -- with a twist that marks a first for both DIGITAL and 
COMDEX. 

DIGITAL will debut the AltaVista airship in flights over the Las 
Vegas Convention Center during the day and over "The Strip" at 
night.  The airship, traveling from Massachusetts to Las Vegas 
during the two weeks prior to the opening of the show, arrives in 
time to inaugurate a brand-new venue for COMDEX exhibit 
demonstrations. 
	  
And on the ground, visitors to DIGITAL's booths in the Convention 
Center will be able to check out and participate in exhibits and 
demos -- with customers and partners -- that showcase the company's 
ability to help enterprise customers develop, deploy, manage, 
migrate and integrate Windows-based solutions more quickly and 
completely than any other vendor.


GET CONNECTED

Booth L4402, North Hall, LVCC

DIGITAL is demonstrating the company's unique strengths and 
capabilities as a provider of Windows enterprise solutions on both 
Intel and Alpha platforms.  Attendees will have an opportunity to 
GET CONNECTED with DIGITAL's latest technology offerings and test 
drive powerful new products designed and optimized for Windows 
NT-based applications. 

DIGITAL is showcasing its full line of PC client/server solutions -- 
ranging from a new Prioris family of aggressively priced, single- 
and dual-processor Pentium Pro servers optimized for Windows NT 4.0 
Server to the company's enterprise-ready Venturis and Celebris 
desktop line-up and HiNote Ultra II and HiNote VP 500 mobile clients 
running Windows NT 4.0 Workstation.  Visitors should look for a 
DIGITAL PC Family presentation on connecting Windows NT to the 
Enterprise in the booth's Enterprise Computing Theater.  

Demos also include high-availability DIGITAL Clusters for Windows NT 
on AlphaServer systems and on Digital StorageWorks Solutions; 
Multivendor StorageWorks Solution with RAID Array 450 dual 
controllers on a SUN SPARC20 running Solaris 2.5; and a UNIX/Windows 
NT demo showing seamless interoperability among Digital UNIX, 
Windows NT and Windows 95 in a manufacturing environment.  
                                  
In addition, three demos running on Digital's Windows NT Personal 
Workstations feature flight simulation by 3DFX; 3D on the Web, a 
client/server VRML demo with an AlphaServer system and Personal 
Workstation client, both running Windows NT and featuring Powerstorm 
Graphics; and Studio MAX by Kinetix, a 3D modeling application 
running on the new Pentium Pro Digital Personal Workstation 200i.

The Interactive Internet Theater highlights the Internet Cyberbrew 
test-drive demo showcasing emerging Internet/Intranet technologies 
for electronic commerce, security and workgroup collaboration.


DIGITAL SEMICONDUCTOR

Booth L4602, North Hall, LVCC

Alpha's superior performance and momentum in the Windows NT market 
is dramatically highlighted by the activities of more than a dozen 
Alpha chip customers and technology partners.  Electronics giants 
Mitsubishi Electric and Samsung Electronics join a host of system 
and component manufacturers to demonstrate new and forthcoming 
systems, ranging from under-$3,000 PCs to dual-processor 500 MHz 
workstations.  A "test drive" area for Digital FX!32 software, 
winner of last year's BYTE magazine "Best New Technology" award, 
demonstrates Alpha's superior performance for all 32-bit Windows 
applications.

DIGITAL's growing merchant vendor business is further evident in the 
