         Digitals Prioris ZX Tops All-time TPC-C Record for
        Microsoft Windows NT Server 4.0 and MS SQL Server 6.5


         DIGITAL Clusters for Windows NT, Record Performance
        and Price/Performance make Prioris ZX the Industry's
           Most Powerful and Reliable SQL Database Server


LAS VEGAS, Nev., Nov. 18, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced record-breaking Transaction Processing Performance 
Council (TPC-C) benchmarks with its award-winning DIGITAL Prioris ZX 
6200MP/4, four-way symmetric multiprocessing Pentium Pro-based 
server.  The system achieved record results of 6,712.53 tpmC at an 
unprecedented $65.16 per tpmC, the lowest cost of ownership ever 
recorded, surpassing the previous price/performance of any 
enterprise server using Windows NT Server 4.0 and SQL Server 6.5, 
by a wide 28 percent margin.

     "DIGITAL's Prioris servers offer customers breakthrough value 
in business-critical IT environments," said Duane Dickhut, vice 
president of DIGITAL's PC Server Business Segment.  "DIGITAL 
Clusters for Windows NT, with seamless failover for Microsoft SQL 
Server 6.5, combined with our Prioris ZX 6000 series, prove that the 
industry's most powerful database PC server is also the industry's 
most available PC server.  The value of our efforts -- to provide 
customers with robust enterprise solutions for Windows NT Server at 
the best cost of ownership -- are clearly demonstrated by today's 
results," he added.

Testing Method

     The TPC-C benchmark is an industry standard, on-line 
transaction processing (OLTP) benchmark that is distributed free by 
the Transaction Processing Performance Council (TPC).  Vendors 
implement and document the tests, which are then reviewed by the 
TPC.  Once audited, the results are submitted to the TPC Council for 
publication.

