           Digital Lowers Desktop Prices Up to 22 Percent


      ...Delivers Leading Price/Performance for the Commercial
                          Desktop Arena...


MAYNARD, Mass., November 19, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today reduced prices by six to 22 percent for its Venturis and 
Celebris Pentium -and Pentium Pro-based product lines.  This move 
provides customers industry-leading price/performance for commercial 
desktop computing.  Estimated U.S. street prices now start at $1,099 
for the entry-level Venturis models.

     According to Steve Keilen, Digital's Director of Desktop 
Product Management and Marketing, "The price reductions throughout 
the line continue to position Digital's desktop PCs in a leadership 
role, offering customers both outstanding price/performance value 
and out-of-the-box networking capability across the enterprise." 

Venturis FX Price Reductions

     Digital continues to deliver price/performance leadership with 
its Venturis models introduced in September.  The company has 
reduced prices by up to 22 percent in the Venturis FX Pentium-based 
line, and 18 percent in the Venturis GL Pentium Pro-based line.  
The estimated street price for the Venturis FX, for example, with a 
133 MHz Intel Pentium processor, 1.2 GB EIDE drive and 16 MB of EDO 
DRAM main memory is now $1,299.  Similarly, a Venturis GL with a 200 
MHz Intel Pentium Pro processor, minitower enclosure, 16 MB of EDO 
DRAM memory, and 2.1 GB EIDE hard drive is now being offered at an 
estimated street price of $2,169, an 18 percent reduction.

Celebris Price Reductions

     Digital also has reduced prices 11 to 19 percent for the 
network-ready Celebris family of products designed for Windows NT in 
the enterprise.  A system with a 133 MHz Intel Pentium processor, 
1.2 GB EIDE drive, 16 MB of EDO DRAM, integrated 10-base-T, and NT 
Cluster-Ready software is now priced at $1,399.

     Pricing for the Celebris GL family, the flagship of Pentium 
Pro-based, "enterprise-ready" systems, has been reduced by 11 to 16 
percent.  Estimated street prices now start at just under $2,300 for 
a configured Pentium Pro 180 MHz processor. 

