MAYNARD, Mass., November 19, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced its next generation of desktop systems design, which 
incorporates design features announced recently by Intel 
Corporation.  This move is in keeping with Digital's commitment to 
provide customers with leading design technology and cost-effective 
enterprise solutions.

     "We are very pleased to be a driving contributor in Intel's NLX 
definition," said Howard Elias, Digital's vice president of 
Worldwide Business Segments, Personal Computer Business Unit. "Many 
of the customer benefits of the new specification -- including easy 
access to the motherboard, disk, and other major system components 
-- are shipping in our desktop products today," he added.

     Intel Corporation recently announced its new motherboard form 
factor, NLX, extending ease of serviceability and upgradeability to 
an industry-standard desktop design. "Digital was valuable in 
helping Intel drive the new NLX specification," said Steve Werner, 
general manager, Intel's OEM Products and Services Division.

     "This is an example of Digital's advanced design capabilities 
setting the trend in the industry, providing leadership technology 
and low cost-of-ownership to our customers," stated Elias.  Overall, 
the new design makes significant advances in the systems' 
manufacturability.  Digital passes that benefit on to customers 
with low cost of ownership and easy servicing.  

     Digital's new enclosure design, first released in the Venturis 
value line earlier this year, is now available across all Digital 
desktops -- from the value line to the enterprise-ready Celebris 
systems, to the recently introduced Personal Workstation line.  In 
addition to the low profile version, Digital extends the technology 
to short tower/desk-side configurations.  

     Digital's desktop lineup, shipping today, also is available for 
viewing and demonstrations at the COMDEX show this week in Las Vegas.

